+## Four-office operating envelope
+
+The latest defensible quantitative planning basis remains the proxy-p95 data
+in `records/economy/2026-11-24-act34-measured-envelope.json`: per office
+11,320,103 accounting tokens, 1,997 wall seconds, and 7,987 seconds of
+conservative whole-host CPU `UPPER_BOUND`; four concurrent offices imply
+45,280,412 tokens, 1,997 elapsed wall seconds (7,988 summed office-wall
+seconds), and 31,948 CPU seconds `UPPER_BOUND`. The CPU figure is deliberately
+not described as attributable office CPU. There is no actual four-office
+sample; peak memory and sustained provider quota remain unavailable.
+
+Current `government/knowledge-budget.conf` permits 1,600,000 aggregate tokens,
+7,200 CPU seconds and 14,400 wall seconds (400,000 / 1,800 / 3,600 per
+office). The proxy p95 is 28.3x the token budget and 4.437x the CPU budget;
+wall fits. Therefore there is no honest proxy-p95-viable four-office envelope
+inside the current caps. A single proxy-p95 wave needs at least the aggregate
+values above; any multi-wave/45-day allowance or headroom is a separate
+Executive/legal decision, bounded by the non-resettable Act34 cumulative
+ledger. These are planning bounds, not a claim of demand or a staffing
+recommendation.
+
+I sent Executive request `P-1795542823-4f91` (irreversible; 24-hour deadline)
+to decide a lawful cap/permit amendment consistent with Act34 or hold. It
+specifically requests no paid-spending authority. All four activation rows
+remain disabled; Economy changed no law, permit, activation state, or live
+model configuration.
